# Directives dropped in here land inside the `reverse_proxy` block, one level
# further in than tls.d's. Empty by default, which is right for a server
# visitors reach directly.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR: another proxy in front of this one — a CDN is the common
# case. The address Caddy sees on the incoming connection is then that
# proxy's, not the visitor's, and Caddy will not believe a forwarded header
# from a peer it has not been told to trust. That refusal is the right
# default: any client can send `X-Forwarded-For`, so believing it from
# everyone would let a visitor choose the address ClickMonk records, counts
# and looks a country up from.
#
# Name the ranges your CDN sends traffic from:
#
#     trusted_proxies 198.51.100.0/24 203.0.113.0/24
#
# That is the `reverse_proxy` sub-directive spelling, which is what belongs in
# this file. `static` is part of Caddy's *global* `servers { trusted_proxies
# static ... }` option, one level up; written here it is read as an IP address
# and Caddy exits at boot.
#
# Your CDN publishes its ranges and they change. A stale list does not fail
# loudly: an unlisted range is simply not believed, and visitors arriving
# through it are recorded as the CDN.
#
# LIST ONLY RANGES YOU ACTUALLY SIT BEHIND. A range covering everything is the
# same as having no check at all: it lets any client claim any address.
#
# This file is not decoration. With an empty directory Caddy logs
# "No files matching import glob pattern" on every start, which reads as a
# misconfiguration to whoever is tailing the logs for the first time.
